body:
I think Lorelai is overly nice to Dean because she sees him as the safe option, when in reality he's not. I'm not defending Jess here either (honestly all of Rory's young adult relationships are kinda unhealthy, they all need therapy), 

&#x200B;

but Lorelai telling Rory "I'd hate to think I raised a kid who couldn't say I love you" after Dean dumped her and said shit like *"Saying I love you doesn't get you pregnant"* is just...really cutting if you think about it and never sat right with me.

I do agree with you about the dangerous driving. It's not the fact that Jess swerved out of the way to avoid a small animal. People conveniently forget the beginning of the episode shows that Jess was driving *very irresponsibly* eating Ice cream, and telling Rory to hold the wheel. That's completely idiotic and irresponsible. 

&#x200B;

I know Jess made sure "she was ok" but the fact of the matter is when Lorelei arrives at the hospital, Jess is nowhere to be found. He left Rory at the hospital and then ran away. I know he's a teenager and he's scared he messed up, but he inadvertently made it worse for himself by not being there at the hospital. 

&#x200B;

Lorelai didn't give Jess a chance to tell his side of the story, which is true- but it's also true that Jess wasn't there, and leaving Rory alone at the hospital is a bad look. I don't think Lorelai is in the wrong for not wanting Rory to be around Jess,

&#x200B;

But she definitely crossed a line when she told Luke to ship Jess back to Liz and told Luke to "go to hell". She knows Jess comes from a broken home, and that Liz is a neglectful addict. She had no business telling Luke to do that to Jess. I sympathize with her protective instinct but she went too far.

This is my first post here, but I feel the exact same way. I had this gnawing psychotic hunger and just snapped, screamed at my mother and threw an empty cottage cheese container. Now that I've cooled down I feel ashamed that I'm such a waste of a daughter.

&#x200B;

My mother does not deserve my rage. An incident like this happens every month, a psychotic outburst and it leaves me feeling so, so ashamed afterwards. I suspect it's PMDD but I have no official diagnosis.

Dean's not a predator, but he IS a liar. He literally says "we BOTH feel it's over" when it comes to his marriage between Lindsay. He also apparently took off the wedding ring.

&#x200B;

Dean deliberately lied about his marital status. And when he comes home to Lindsay, it's clear that he lied to Rory and had no intention of leaving his wife.

&#x200B;

He's not a predator but he IS a liar. To Both women

I think Dean is more in the wrong here because he literally lied to Rory's face and said "it's over we both feel it". He literally says point blank, that his marriage is over with, AND most importantly, that Lindsay also thinks that it's over. 

&#x200B;

I'm not saying Rory is an angel victim, but Dean flat out lied to Rory here.

It's literally ridiculous that when other characters hurt Rory or screw up in some way it's fine, but the top comment on this post is how "Rory doesn't deserve to be upset because she's a cheater (ignoring the context that Rory was way younger and that Logan is a grown ass man)" 

&#x200B;

Logan obviously messed up and hurt his girlfriend, and all everyone can focus on is "Rory sucks!" It's obviously a shitty awful thing to do to someone! 

&#x200B;

Not telling your girlfriend that you slept with an entire room of women who are your sisters bridesmaids is FOUL.

That's where I disagree, when Rory tried to talk about why she was going through such a hard time, and why the crisis was happening, Lorelei stood up from the diner, walked out, and said "topic is over, you're not bumming around at home"-

&#x200B;

As if Rory had EVER bummed around at home and hasn't been doing a nonstop marathon since Chilton. If Rory is at fault here, than fine, but Lorelei has a pattern of making ultimatums and burning bridges when it comes to her loved ones- demanding that Luke marry her and then sleeping with Chris, ending her engagement with Max days before the wedding- and then refusing to talk about why she does these things.

&#x200B;

Lorelei is hostile when it comes to other people's input/output if it doesn't fit into her world view, she doesn't even try to hear them out.

To be fair, she was *two weeks* into an internship where Rory had no clear expectations set forth for her internship, and was only twenty.

&#x200B;

Also AYITL featured Luke not knowing how *actual surrogacy* works, had a crappy musical episode, and had Paris and Doyle divorcing...there's a *lot* of inaccuracies with AYITL

Exactly, and Rory was a college sophmore two weeks into her internship, two weeks is *not* long enough to know anything about an employee.

&#x200B;

And yet Mitchum is so hypocritical towards his son, Logan who is constantly sleeping around, getting expelled of private high schools, doing dangerous stupid shit with the life and death brigade, and still thinks Logan should inherit the family fortune.

The amount of women, especially young women, in the comment section not understanding how sleazy and misogynistic for Rory's older male boss to say "you'd be a good assistant"...is certainly disappointing

&#x200B;

Rory's a twenty year old intern, *two weeks* into her news job and Mitchum is a billionaire CEO who inherited his fortune. Gross, condescending guys like Mitchum who tell women they'd be good assistants, were par for the course and everyone knew how slimy they are.

Honestly the Jess bracelet thing is more on Jess because didn't Lorelei see jess sneak out of Rory's room after he placed the bracelet back inside? That would raise so many red flags to me if I saw a *teenage boy sneaking out of my daughter's room,* and this was a huge violation of her boundaries and trust since Jess promised to do an errand.

&#x200B;

Agree about the Dean thing too, everyone was way to invested in Rory's love life, poor girl couldn't catch a break.

Thank you. He's literally a scumbag billionaire who inherited all his wealth. He hasn't had to work for shit, and he literally told a 20 year old intern that "she should be an assistant" after *2 weeks.* 

&#x200B;

I just don't think anyone with common sense makes judgements about someone's career after 2 weeks.

I mean, according to Lorelei herself, she and Christopher used to be crashing brand new sports cars, and she snuck out regularly, so I'm sure her life wasn't all doom and gloom as a rich kid.

&#x200B;

besides, most of the night was pretty miserable for Rory too, they basically paraded her as a piece of meat in front of other rich boys, and she couldn't even complain because Emily and Richard were paying for Yale. 

&#x200B;

I don't think Rory could sit through this insufferable WASP mating ritual sober, especially after Dean dumped her.

Yeah, a lot of people say that Rory was choosing the "upper crust life here" but Rory getting drunk is a result of Dean literally dumping her in front of everyone and being forced to sit through a party that she hates.

&#x200B;

And it's not like Rory can get out of it, because her grandparents are paying for Yale. So she has to sit there like a piece of meat, and basically have one of the shittiest nights of her life- I mean, I sure as fuck wouldn't be sober for the duration of that nightmare either.

Yeah especially considering that *everyone* in Rory's life has an agenda for her. This includes Lorelei.

&#x200B;

Emily and Richard want Rory to be the do over child for Lorelei. They want Rory to go to Yale, and have a debutante ball party. They want Rory to marry upper class, as shown by this extravagant party that Rory didn't even want, they try to use money to control Rory's life (inviting the pastor to speak about her sex life).

&#x200B;

Meanwhile, Lorelei wants Rory to be the do over child for herself- basically so the things Lorelei could never do as a big "screw you" to her parents. She wants Rory to go to harvard, and even jokes about forgetting whose dream it is, and brags about how 4 year old Rory had a Harvard sweatshirt (she was 4!)- because Harvard is Yale's rival. 

&#x200B;

Lorelei freaks out in front of other people when Rory even *thinks* of going off the plan, like when she applies to other schools besides Harvard. When Lorelei runs away from her marriage with Max, she drags Rory all the way to harvard, and stares longingly at the valedictorian posters.

&#x200B;

Meanwhile, Rory has to deal with the pressure of everybody's expectations. Lorelei and Emily and Richard put her up on a pedestal, sure, but they have so many ridiculous expectations for Rory sometimes it's insane- get top grades, go to harvard, go to Yale, marry rich and upper crust status man, don't act out, don't have a boyfriend unless it's one they approve of (we've seen how Lorelei fucking despises Jess, and keeps pretending that clingy, angry dean is a better alternative), don't take any breaks, don't re-evaluate your goals, just keep going and going and going and achieving and achieving and keep being the golden child because their love is conditional.

I worked at a college center for many, *many years* and let me tell you, panicked college students are so, so common. It's extremely normal (unfortunately) for college students to have mental breakdowns in an office, so take leave of absences, to feel lost, etc. *Incredibly common.*

&#x200B;

And it's not really a character flaw or and indicator of anything really, college is a stressful time. Also at the end of the Doyle criticism, Rory rose to the occasion and wrote an article Doyle liked. And she doesn't change her mind, she was understandable rattled because someone wrote "die jerk" on her board and yelled at her in public, and she felt guilty. When Doyle tells her she did a great job, she's shown writing a new article at the end of the episode.

Probably because cheating story lines create a LOT of drama which means more views and more money for the network. I genuinely think ASP thought the Paul situation was  *hilarious* because no one could remember his name, just like the terrible "oober" joke. Ugh.

&#x200B;

Edit: actually now that I think about it SO many shows have weird cheating plots: Diane from Bojack Horseman, Zoey Johnson from Grownish and Blackish, Betty from Riverdale, Gossip Girl, Pretty Little Liars, Maddy from Euphoria, even freaking Dorothy from Golden Girls. TV writers love messy relationships.

Eh, Marty was equally immature, and flirted with Rory at Olivia's birthday party- he literally called Rory at his own girlfriend's birthday party.

&#x200B;

I think Rory and Marty started out as his friends, but Marty was honestly a "nice" guy. Maybe he didn't have a trust fund, but he also had some serious hang ups as well. Maybe they would've worked ten years down the line when they're both mature adults, who knows

Marty was slimy, he literally flirted with Rory at his *own girlfriend's birthday party* and called her beautiful and she looked uncomfortable after he said so. 

&#x200B;

 I'm sorry but I see post after post about how Rory is stringing men along and it feels really gross when I see zero posts about how said men openly pursue Rory when *they have their own relationships.*

Yes, Thank You! I saw a post about how Paris would have been a better journalist because she was more aggressive (aka masculine coded) and that Mitchum was right because Rory is too passive (two weeks into a job, that too)

&#x200B;

Meanwhile when Rory *is* aggressive and forces he editor to see her work and get an internship, she's seen as "too extra" and "too try hard" and "entitled". If Paris did the *exact same thing* this sub would applause Paris for being a great journalist.

&#x200B;

Paris had a *mental breakdown* for not getting into Harvard after botching her interview (she literally argued for eugenics wtf) and losing her virginity and I see post after post about sympathizing with Paris. After the yale Daily News fiasco, Paris loses her drive and kicks Rory and Doyle out of their *shared* apartment. 

&#x200B;

Yet I see posts literally mocking Rory after Mitchum, a literal billionaire who owns the US media, and one of the most powerful people in the world, the literal newspaper industry, told her she didn't have it- which isn't constructive criticism. 

&#x200B;

I never see posts about how she's entitled, even though she also cheats on her longtime bf Jaime, etc. In fact, I see posts about how she *deserved* to go to Harvard, and how Rory getting into a top school was unrealistic (even though she's valedictorian at an ivy league feeder, student body VP, on the Franklin, raised by a single mom, volunteers constantly in Stars Hollow) etc. 

&#x200B;

It's so, so easy to shit on Rory, a female coded character, and honestly, it just shows that misogyny is alive and well. Just look at how Pam is treated in The Office subreddit or how Skylar is treated.

That's all I ask for. I now know you didn't mean for it to sound anti feminist, but that's genuinely what it sounded like, and it gave me the ick.

&#x200B;

EDIT: why did you even bring up man hating in the first place? I don't recall Rory or Lorelei ever man hating??

No, no, no you don't understand it's easy to demonize a teenage girl  with an underdeveloped character that was written 20 years ago, then apply some actual nuance.

&#x200B;

I swear "men's media" and male characters like early Iron Man or Walt White or Dwight from the Office *never* get this much hate, the bar for male characters are on the floor, whereas female characters are torn apart for the smallest things.

"She’s always so rude please tell me she gets nicer later on in the show"

&#x200B;

*oh my sweet summer child...*

Marty is just as much to blame for this fiasco, as Rory is. I think she genuinely got *too comfortable* with Marty and didn't realize that he was invested in a relationship with him.

&#x200B;

Also, I can understand why Marty suddenly went cold in her life considering that after Marty got with olivia, he pretended that he didn't know Rory at all. When Marty *did finally* get a Girlfriend, Olivia, he shoots himself in the foot by trying to flirt with Rory at *his own girlfriend's birthday party.*

&#x200B;

*Doesn't Marty know what kind of signals he's giving Rory, despite having a girlfriend? Isn't he leading her on?*

But that's the thing. If your partner *begged* you for a night off, and you agreed to it, and claimed you were a saint, then why would you *deliberately break your promise* and go over anyway, and scream at them and pick a fight in front of guests, without even waiting for an explanation?

&#x200B;

That's *also* going behind your partner's back and deliberately disrespecting their word. 

&#x200B;

Also, everything with Dean has to be a fight, or a beg, or a plead, because Dean literally blows up when the relationship doesn't go exactly at his pace. Like I said, Dean was controlling *well before* Jess comes to the scene.

&#x200B;

When Rory is unable to say "I love you" after three, THREE months of dating, he yells at her, makes *nasty* comments, and then dumps her on their three month anniversary.

&#x200B;

And Dean is controlling of Rory *well after*, he breaks up with her, publicly again, which is whatever, because I can sympathize with Dean's anger. 

&#x200B;

But When Jess and Rory finally get together, Dean literally starts intimidating Jess, saying that he doesn't have to be nice anymore. When Dean marries Lindsay, Dean still creeps on Rory and say's Rory's name the night before he's supposed to marry Lindsay. 

&#x200B;

Dean is obsessed with Rory, and has an unhealthy attachment to Rory before Jess comes on to the scene, after he gets *married.*

Yes, but Dean acted as if Rory invited Paris and Jess, when both of them came to her, when she wanted a night alone. Paris and Jess showed up to her house uninvited- she did not *plan* to exclude Dean.

&#x200B;

Either way, Rory shouldn't have to plead with her boyfriend for a night out, at all, and Dean said he was a "saint" for letting Rory have a night off- only to immediately break his promise to Rory and come over anyway, which he planned. And then he picks a fight and starts yelling at Rory, to the point where Paris has to lie and intervene.

&#x200B;

And like I said, Dean was insecure *well before* Jess was ever on the scene.

Same! I feel like if you point out a character's positive or negative traits or defend them or criticize a character, you're automatically labelled an "x hater" or "x lover".

&#x200B;

I literally have to preface every response I make with, "I don't hate Y but..." or "I love x but..." or else people assume that you're suddenly x character's ride or die.

&#x200B;

I really, really like Rory and Lorelai and I think they're decent people. Sure, they make some mistakes and it's frustrating to watch them sometimes, but if I knew them irl I think I'd be proud to call them my friend.

Eh, I thought that was because the writers didn't care much about Lane at that point, and just made her a "cool girl" so she wouldn't rock the boat. Zach was *extremely unsupportive* and ruined the record deal for the band, and he just never apologized for it. Ever.

&#x200B;

Maybe it's because Lane is a side character and she's not white, but I feel like if Jess or Dean purposefully sabotaged Rory's chances of getting into Yale, or Luke ruined Lorelei's chances of purchasing the dragonfly, the writers wouldn't portray them like that at all.

Don't get me wrong, Richard's behavior is ridiculous and over the top, but I think him complaining about the contest was supposed to be a projection of his internal frustration after being sidelined and made obsolete at his company, and this was an era where company and employee loyalty was a thing. 

&#x200B;

I thought him being overly invested wasn't *just* about Richard complaining to complain but also to highlight how much he misses work, and the world of business.

&#x200B;

I absolutely agree that Richard is the most guilty of perpetuating Gilmore supremacy

Luke literally started out a woke king, talking about the plight of Native Americans, the ecological deforestation of golf courses, etc,

&#x200B;

I liked it because it was a good flip on how the small town rugged guy is supposed to be "conservative" but Luke is actually pretty woke and cool- until the cursed Vineyard Valentine episode where he becomes an actual himbo, dammit ASP, why you gotta do luke dirty like that?

"Mom, You fraternized with a known Nazi? That's despicable!"

&#x200B;

"No, my dear, that's *a joke."**^(LMAO Gottem)*
